# Deployment and routing

- Target is Ubuntu 24.04 with NVIDIA Container Toolkit and pinned/tested driver, CUDA, container, and ML compatibility versions.
- Docker Compose is acceptable for the initial single-node application; keep manifests structured for later service separation.
- Only Nginx publishes 80/443. PostgreSQL, MySQL, MinIO, NATS, Redis/Dragonfly, parsers, model servers, Studio, and admin tools remain internal.
- Routing: `/` and WordPress paths → WordPress; `/app` → Next.js; `/app/api/v1` → Go backend.
- Do not expose Unsloth Studio publicly. If web access is required, place it behind separate admin authentication and network controls.
- TLS redirects HTTP, enables HSTS after validation, uses modern ciphers, and automates renewal without exposing secrets.
- Apply request/body/time limits per route; streaming chat disables proxy buffering and has bounded idle/total timeouts.
- Containers run non-root where supported, read-only filesystem where practical, dropped capabilities, no privileged mode, and scoped volumes.
- Never mount Docker socket into application/AI containers.
- Separate networks for public edge, application, data, and ML services where Compose permits.
- Pin image versions/digests in production; do not use `latest`.
- Persist only declared volumes. Back up data before migrations and validate free disk/VRAM before model operations.
- Health checks distinguish liveness from readiness; model readiness means the version is loaded and can serve a bounded probe.
- Deploy backward-compatible schema first, then services, then remove old paths. Keep previous images/config for rollback.
- Secrets come from server secret management/environment, never Compose literals or repository files.
- WordPress has separate DB credentials, filesystem, update policy, plugins, backups, and no access to corporate PostgreSQL/MinIO.
- No production deploy, migration, certificate change, DNS change, or model promotion without explicit user request.
